  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nLow clouds are still struggling to form early this morning over\nthe coastal waters. The marine layer will remain thin over the\nnext couple of days, bringing low clouds and patchy fog to areas \nwithin around 10 miles of the coast each late night and morning. \nOne of the reasons for this will be high pressure aloft over \nMexico beginning to expand further into our region during this \ntime, bringing slightly warmer weather. With highs in the 90s for \nthe lower deserts yesterday, there is a very high chance (85+ %) \nto see highs eclipse 100 degrees today and Saturday. Valleys west \nof the mountains will see the largest warm up of around 5 degrees \nwith highs in the 90s from Escondido to Temecula to Ontario.\n\nModel trends beginning to come together on a deep trough dropping\nin from the north by Sunday and becoming a closed low by Monday. \nAs it does so, Hurricane Polo moves north on Sunday and \nnortheastward on Monday, where it is expected to make landfall \nsomewhere in southern Baja California later on Monday. The \ntroughing pattern will act as a mechanism to help pull in some of \nthis tropical moisture, mainly in the form of clouds and humidity.\n\nThe key will be where exactly our low pressure system sets up. \nModels continue to push the system slightly further to the south, \nwhich would not entrain as much moisture. Two things look more \ncertain however: increased humidity (though high temperatures \nwill not be as hot as today and Saturday), and increased swell and\ncoastal flooding/erosion. Warmer nights can be expected near the \ncoast with some places staying above 70 degrees. Monday will be \nthe coolest day overall with highs in the 70s and 80s from the \ncoastal to the foothills as well as the high desert. NBM gives \nPalm Springs only a 50-50 chance to reach over 90 degrees on\nMonday as well.\n\nThough moisture increases are seen with PWAT values exceeding one\ninch, the low's track will be important on determining any\naccumulating rainfall for area. Moisture in the form of high \nclouds may spread over SoCal during this time, but lack of forcing\nmechanisms may only provide us cloudier, humid days. These clouds\nmay also inhibit max temperatures as well.\n\nThe area of low pressure should begin to move out of the region by\nTuesday. Depending on its location, offshore flow may accompany\nthis exit. The strength is still uncertain as well as how hot we \nbecome by Tuesday and Wednesday. This is seen in quite the large \nspread in temperature guidance. Take the Miramar Base in San \nDiego: near a 15 degree spread in high temperatures from \n25th-75th percentiles with highs in the upper 70s to lower 90s. \nWith these factors, confidence is high that there will be less \nhumidity and warmer temperatures, just not sure how warm. Weak \nhigh pressure looks to stick around into at least the middle of \nnext week, keeping temperatures slightly above normal west of the \nmountains and closer to normal for the mountains and deserts.",
